course: dictionary
lesson: 14508
title: Penetrate: going in, getting in, understanding
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Penetrate is a verb. Its main meaning is to go into something or through it, often with effort. «The sharp nail penetrated the old wood.» The nail pushed its way in, so penetrated must mean went into or through.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Sunlight can penetrate clear water, so we can see the fish.» The sentence says we can see the fish, so the light must go down into the water. Swap in your meaning: the light went into the water. It still makes sense.
explain: Penetrate can also mean getting into a group or a market, or understanding something hidden. «The new brand finally penetrated the market.» The brand got in. «Her clever question helped us penetrate the mystery.» Her question helped us understand it.
fact: Penetrate means to go into or through something, often with effort.
fact: Penetrate can mean to get into a group or market successfully.
fact: In a more formal sense, to penetrate an idea or mystery means to understand it.
fact: Penetrate is a verb: penetrate, penetrates, penetrated, penetrating.
fact: In science, light penetrates water, X-rays penetrate the body, and roots penetrate the soil.
exercise: «The sharp nail penetrated the old wood.» What does penetrated mean here? | Painted over the surface | Fell off the table | Was cut into small pieces | * Went into and through the wood
exercise: «Sunlight can penetrate clear water.» What does penetrate mean here? | Turn the water to ice | * Go into the water | Change colour | Bounce off the top
exercise: «Her clever question helped us penetrate the mystery.» What does penetrate mean here? | Forget | * Understand | Hide from | Ignore
test: «The new brand finally penetrated the market.» What does penetrated mean here? | Sold all its goods in one day | Left the market in a hurry | Stood outside the market | * Got into the market successfully
test: «Her eyes could not penetrate the darkness.» What does this tell you? | * She could not see through the darkness | The darkness was made of light | She closed her eyes to sleep | She could see very clearly in the dark
exam: «The rain could not penetrate the thick roof of the tent.» What does penetrate mean? | Shine on | Drip down | * Get through | Stay outside
